From 26f299efa8f1acb19412bb86a54b683905992fcc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 11:23:59 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 084/206] feat(shorebird): print_shorebird_info reproduced,
 both passes

The object pool half closes the flag. Its link-results producer is the same
shape as the class table's -- 0x10019bdc4 strides 0x60 = sizeof(CodeInfo) and
reads the string at +0x18, which is op_subgraph_hash_, not self_hash_ at +0x00
-- but what it emits is pairs of pool indices rather than pairs of records, so
each surviving CodeInfo pair contributes the element-wise pairing of the two
sides' index vectors, deduplicated.

That last step was not readable and was settled by arithmetic instead. Grouping
by self_hash gives 1089 pairs and by subgraph hash 1378, against their reported
1121; the deduplicated element-wise expansion of the subgraph-keyed pairs gives
exactly 1121. Running all three stages on it then reproduces 1113 / 292 / 16,
which sums to 1421, the pool size.

Also added: --base_op_link_data and --patch_op_link_data, whose names and
mismatch error come from their binary's own strings, ReadObjectPoolLinkInfo,
and real base_object_pool_ / patch_object_pool_ fields where the header had
sixteen reserved bytes.

With both passes wired, our report is identical to theirs on their own
link-info files, all eight lines:

  606 / 606 / 0 / 13     and     1121 / 1113 / 292 / 16

shorebird_info_check.sh holds both, and runs two inputs rather than one: the
fixture's base.ct and patch.ct are byte-identical, so on their own they never
exercise the unpaired path. The second input drops 20 classes and moves the
class table numbers to 586 / 33, where we also match.

One thing is deliberately not claimed. self_pp_indices_, subgraph_pp_indices_
and their union all yield the same 1121 on this fixture, so it cannot say which
vector they use. self_pp_indices_ is used and the header says plainly that the
choice is unverified.

verify_all.sh: 6 ran, 0 failed, 0 skipped, 113 unit tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

